Falsifies `system_tests::user::terminal_quiesce_tests::a_quiesce_from_anyone_but_the_supervisor_is_refused_and_the_terminal_serves_on`.

Architecture: aarch64

The patch serves `OPERATION_QUIESCE` again on the unbadged copy the shell, `login`, the input
driver and the spawner hold, by admitting it in `line_editor`'s serve loop beside
`terminal_contract::admits`. A supervised `line_editor` then honors a stranger's quiesce: it hands
the parked reader back `FLAG_RETRY`, answers `QUIESCED` and stops on its control endpoint, where
only the supervisor speaks.

Expected to fail: `assert_eq!` "an unbadged holder quiesced a supervised terminal (reply
0x51554954)", with `left: 1364543828` (`proto::QUIESCED`). The patch sits in the serve loop because
the table itself left the tree with `terminal_contract`, whose own host test
`only_the_supervisor_badge_admits_a_quiesce` pins the table. Replayed red on aarch64 on 2026-11-22
(UTC).

diff --git a/components/src/line_editor.rs b/components/src/line_editor.rs
index 71b08e4ce..a93a0af04 100644
--- a/components/src/line_editor.rs
+++ b/components/src/line_editor.rs
@@ -159,7 +159,7 @@ fn start(mode: u64, control: u64, start: u64) -> ! {
             reply(slot, proto::BAD_REQUEST, 0);
             continue;
         };
-        if !proto::admits(badge, operation) {
+        if !(proto::admits(badge, operation) || (badge == 0 && operation == proto::OPERATION_QUIESCE)) {
             reply(slot, proto::BAD_REQUEST, 0);
             continue;
         }
